{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target asserts that reinstatement remains a derived property, but this does not hold for the proposed intersection semantics. Under standard grounded semantics, if all of a conjecture's attackers are refuted and it has no unrefuted defenders blocking it, the conjecture is reinstated and accepted. In the graded multi-threshold scheme, a conjecture can be IN at the lowest threshold (legitimately reinstated there), have its attackers refuted at all thresholds, yet be rejected overall because it is OUT at a higher threshold due to a newly admissible high-strength attacker. This breaks reinstatement as a derived property of the composite semantics, violating a required guarantee.","counterexample":[{"attackers":[{"id":"a1","strength":0.4},{"id":"a2","strength":0.9}],"composite":"c=OUT","conjecture":"c","result_at_0.3":{"a1":"OUT","a2":"OUT","c":"IN"},"result_at_0.8":{"a1":"OUT","a2":"IN","c":"OUT"},"thresholds":[0.3,0.8]}],"target":"f60a3dfe1afd977779ec09c7dcdfc4b5d26ebd2e40fa612d39f2d13052f76795"}]}